// Wild species that people can gather, hunt and (from M2) domesticate.
//
// Each entry is an archetype of the wild ancestors of a domesticated crop or
// animal. The niche describes where it thrives, as trapezoids
// [min, best_from, best_to, max]: suitability is 0 outside min..max, 1 between
// best_from and best_to, and linear in between.
//
//   coldest_month_c, warmest_month_c  mean temperature of those months, °C
//   annual_precip_mm                  yearly rainfall, mm
//   rain_season                       -1 = all rain in winter (Mediterranean),
//                                     0 = even, +1 = all rain in summer (monsoon)
//   elevation_m                       metres above sea level
//   river_affinity                    0-1: how much it depends on river floodplains
//
// Real species live in a few native regions, not everywhere the climate
// suits them. Each world places `native_regions` homelands among suitable
// land, at least `range_km` apart, and the species spreads up to `range_km`
// from each through suitable country.
//
// domesticability (0-1) is how readily people can tame or cultivate the
// species; food_value (0-1) how much food it gives once domesticated, relative
// to the best staple crops. `domesticated` names the domesticated form.
//
// Domesticated, a species grows beyond its wild niche: farmers can raise it
// wherever the niche widened by 4 °C on each temperature, 30% on rainfall and
// 500 m on elevation still fits.
//
// domesticated_before_start: already domesticated when a run begins (dogs,
// tamed by foragers long before farming): known wherever the species is
// native, and spread by contact from there.
